 { Welcome to this 2024 edition of Chess Structures - A Grandmaster Guide. I am Grandmaster Peter Heine Nielsen and I am happy to present this new, updated Chessable edition to you.The original book Chess Structures was written by GM Mauricio Flores Rios and published in 2015, and imported to Chessable in early 2019. Since then, the rise of new modern engines, especially the neural network (NN) ones like AlphaZero and the most recent Stockfish versions, has significantly altered our perception of many chess openings. This has ramifications for pawn structures as well, and that is what I would like to discuss in this updated version of the course.To give a concrete example of how our understanding has changed, I wanted to show a game by Magnus Carlsen (who I've coached for many years) which he played at the World Rapid Championship in 2022 against Nodirbek Abdusattorov. } 1. { -KEY- } b3 $5 { The Nimzowitsch-Larsen opening has seen a surge in popularity recently, especially due to a popular Chessable LTR by Adhiban, dubbing his followers '1.b3 cowboys'. As a Dane I'll stick to its original name, and I'd also like to point out that while Magnus did score an impressive 4.5/5 points with this move in the World Rapid and blitz, Larsen actually scored 6/6 with it in Teeside 1972!What's the point of this modest looking move? According to the engines it immediately spurns any hope of a white advantage, though it 'merely' drops the evaluation to 0.00. And thats exactly the point. It gives White a position that is not worse, and encourages Black to not play symmetrically but instead start playing in the centre. It is a move for confident players, believing they are better than their opponents. I recall asking Larsen about a typical position, telling him: 'but you are not objectively better here', getting the reply: 'No, but then I will have to beat him by playing better!' } e5 { The most logical reply, occupying the centre and blocking the bishop's diagonal at the same time. } 2. Bb2 Nc6 3. e3 Nf6 4. Nf3 $5 ( { 50 years ago, Larsen used } 4. Bb5 { to beat fellow Scandinavian Andersson, but later it has been established that after } Bd6 $1 { Black is perfectly fine, the point being that } 5. Na3 { is met strongly by } Na5 $1 ) 
Bd6 $5 ( { Abdusattorov copies the concept, but now at least White does not have a bishop on the unnatural b5-square. } 4... e4 { is more principled, and Magnus later faced it and won in an important blitz game against the later bronze winner Martirosyan. With } e4 { Black gains some time for development, but at least White has managed to re-open the diagonal for the b2-bishop. } ) 
5. c4 O-O 6. d3 Re8 7. a3 a5 8. Be2 Bf8 9. O-O d5 10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Nbd2 { A typical reversed Sicilian structure has arisen, 'typical' meaning that White has gotten an ideal setup. This is because White, with reversed colors, has an extra tempo compared to normal Sicilians, and thus can fight for the initiative in several agressive ways.Here, Abdusattorov had to settle for just making standard moves, achieving a solid position. From the reversed Dragon we know that even with an extra tempo, White, having avoided the sharper tries, does not necessarily get a better position. } f6 12. Qc2 Bf5 { 12...Be6 was played in Fischer-Andersson, Siegen 1970 (!). Abdusattorov chooses a different location for his bishop. } ( { The Fischer-Andersson game continued interestingly: } 12... Be6 13. Kh1 $5 Qd7 14. Rg1 Rad8 15. Ne4 Qf7 16. g4 g6 17. Rg3 Bg7 18. Rag1 Nb6 19. Nc5 Bc8 20. Nh4 Nd7 21. Ne4 Nf8 22. Nf5 { Fischer had a nice position and won a good game. } ) 
13. Rfe1 Bg6 { With colors reversed the plan would be Ne4, Rac1, and a later d4, equalizing comfortably, but now, having the white pieces, this seems less ambitious.The concept we will see next is the same one as in the brilliant Fischer-Andersson mentioned above. There Fischer had to make preparatory moves like Kh1 and Rg1, due to the g4 square being protected by Black, but here Magnus can execute the plan right away: } 14. g4 $5 { Objectively this should get a question mark, but the concept was played by both Fischer and Carlsen, and has been praised by Kasparov, which you can argue overrules the computer. } ( { And indeed, the main problem is a 'random tactic', as Black is better after } 14. g4 a4 $1 { The point is that } 15. b4 Ncxb4 16. axb4 Nxb4 { gives Black 3 pawns for the piece under very favourable circumstances, as now the fact that the g-pawn is on g4 really hurts White's position. } 15. bxa4 { is a better choice than 15.b4, but after } Nb6 $1 { Black regains the pawn and White does not really have a good way to easily regroup. Again the pawn on g4 feels misplaced. } ) 
Qd7 15. Kh1 Rad8 $6 { An obvious 'logical' move, but a bad one! This is why the white setup is so attractive - the logical flow of the game helps steer it in his preferred direction. } ( { However, modern computers indicate a much stronger plan for Black: } 15... h6 $1 { stops White's immediate intentions on the kingside, and after } 16. Rg1 Nd8 17. h4 Nf7 18. Rg2 Ra6 $1 { Black has not only consolidated his kingside in a harmonious manner, but the rook on a6 is excellently placed too. It does not only defend via the 6th rank but also intends }  Rc6 { , when the knight threatens to enter on c3. If anything it is Black who is better, but mainly it becomes a 2-way complex struggle. } ) 
16. Ne4 Kh8 17. Rad1 Bf7 18. Rg1 { Abdusattorov's main problem is that his position is 'perfect'. All his pieces are on ideal squares, yet nothing concrete can be done. And if he just waits, White will keep making useful moves, improving his own position. This is a strange paradox, pointed out by Mihai Suba when he explained these types of Scheveningen and Hedgehog-style positions. } Nb6 19. Rg3 ( { It is interesting how modern computers treat certain chess positions. Here, without any hesitation, the computer prefers } 19. g5 f5 20. g6 $1 Bxg6 21. Neg5 { . There is no immediate reward for White's pawn sac - White is not winning, he isn't even considerably better, but the computer simply prefers White's long-term attacking chances, even a pawn down. Nh4 might be next, then doubling on the g-file, etc. It is easy to see why it is attactive, yet White is a full pawn down and has no immediate threats. } ) 
a4 $5 { Not necessarily a good move, but a very human one. Black needs to generate counterplay and can't just sit and wait. } 20. bxa4 Na5 ( { At least now Magnus faces directs threats and has to react. But apparently this move contains a strong element of bluff, as both } 20... Na5 20. g5 Bb3 22. Qc1 Bxd1 23. Bxd1 $1 { and } 21. Qb1 Nxa4 22. g5 f5 23. g6 $1 { are better for White. However, Magnus doesn't immediately manage to make the necessary mental shift, and keeps regrouping: } ) 
21. Rdg1 $6 Bd5 $6 ( { Here, however, Abdusattorov slows down and thus misses his chance: } 21... Nxa4 $1 { was the move, the big difference being that after } 22. g5 f5 23. g6 { the move } Bb3 $1 { is available, ensuring that the kingside remains closed while Black has made considerable progress on the queenside. } ) 
22. g5 f5 23. Nc3 ( { Not a bad move, and White is still better after this, but there was a brilliant alternative. } 23. Nf6 $3 { wins, as after } gxf6 24. gxf6 { Black has no reasonable way to defend against White's plan of e4 closing the long diagonal, after which Nxe5 becomes unstoppable. } Qc6 { is met by } 25. Qb1 $1 { and after } Qxf6 26. e4 { it is striking that, although White's attack perhaps seems a bit slow, the computer already gives a +3 evaluation despite the fact that White is a piece down for the moment! } ) 
Bc6 24. e4 Nxa4 25. Nxa4 Bxa4 26. Qc3 Nc6 { Abdusattorov has regained the pawn, and his positional trumps are obvious. This is the crucial stage of the game: Magnus has to find the precise attacking moves, as otherwise Black is just better. } 27. g6 $1 ( { Brilliant! Even if the brilliance is somewhat hidden. The intention is obvious: White vacates the g5-square, 28.Ng5 now being the main threat. } 27. g6 h6 { does not prevent it, as after } 28. Ng5 $1 hxg5 29. Rh3 Kg8 { White plays } 30. d4 $3 { , which is more than the black position can take. It threatens not only }  31. Bc4 { , but also } 31. Rh8 Kxh8 32. Qh3 { with mate next move on h7. } ) 
 ( { If } 27. g6 f4 { there are several solutions, but } 28. Rh3 { (!) } Qxh3 29. Ng5 Qd7 30. Nf7 Kg8 31. Qc4 { is the most extraordinary, } Re6 { being answered by } 32. Bg4 $1 { and } Qe6 { by } 32. gxh7 $1 { , either queening or forking the queen with a check on g5 next, winning immediately.Abdusattorov chooses the toughest defence available: } ) 
Nd4 $5 28. Ng5 $1 ( { There is no time for hesitation, no time for details like defending the bishop which is hanging on e2. } 28. Ng5 Re7 { was technically Black's best reply, even though White is winning after the interesting } 29. Bh5 $1 { , securing access to the f7-square. Of course, Abdusattotov takes the offered piece: } ) 
Nxe2 29. Nf7+ $6 ( { A much cleaner, and more beautiful win would have been } 29. Qc4 $1 { , as after } Nxg3 30. Rxg3 { , despite being a rook down, White's threats are unstoppable. perhaps Magnus feared } Re7 { , covering the f7-square and stopping the knight check, but then } 31. gxh7 $1 { sets up the threat of }  32. Qg8 { mate, which can only be prevented by Black giving up large amounts of material. } ) 
Qxf7 30. gxf7 Nxc3 31. fxe8=Q Bxe8 $2 ( { A strange mistake, as this leaves the e5-pawn hanging. } 31... Rxe8 32. Bxc3 Bc6 { still puts up resistance. But both players had almost no time left at this point. } ) 
32. Bxc3 Bg6 33. exf5 Bxf5 34. Bxe5 Bxd3 35. Rxg7 $1 ( { Since after } 35. Rxg7 Bxg7 { White can choose between } 36. Bxg7 Kg8 37. Bf6 { or just } 36. Rxg7 { , both winning instantly, Abdusattorov resigned. I think this game is a good example both of how 'typical' plans for certain pawn structures are executed in today's top games, as well as how modern computers can enrich our understanding of these structures.Have fun studying Chess Structures - A Grandmaster Guide!Peter Heine Nielsen, Vilnius 2024 } { _ } ) 

 1-0

